1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,576
What if I told you that right now, at

2
00:00:03,576 --> 00:00:07,152
this very second, our entire galaxy is being dragged

3
00:00:07,152 --> 00:00:10,728
through space toward something we cannot see? Not drifting.

4
00:00:10,728 --> 00:00:14,304
Dragged. The Milky Way is rushing at six hundred

5
00:00:14,304 --> 00:00:17,880
kilometers every second toward a point in the universe

6
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:21,456
that is completely invisible to us. So what is

7
00:00:21,456 --> 00:00:25,032
pulling us? Astronomers have measured it. They have mapped

8
00:00:25,032 --> 00:00:28,608
it. And the more precisely they measure, the less

9
00:00:28,608 --> 00:00:32,184
sense it makes.

10
00:00:33,684 --> 00:00:37,392
Six hundred kilometers per second. Let that number sink in.

11
00:00:37,392 --> 00:00:41,100
That is over two million kilometers every hour, faster than

12
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:44,808
anything humans have ever built. And we are not the

13
00:00:44,808 --> 00:00:48,516
only ones moving. Tens of thousands of galaxies are all

14
00:00:48,516 --> 00:00:52,224
streaming in the same direction, like leaves caught in the

15
00:00:52,224 --> 00:00:55,932
same invisible river. To cross our own galaxy at that

16
00:00:55,932 --> 00:00:59,640
speed would still take thousands of years, yet on the

17
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:03,348
cosmic scale it is a sprint.

18
00:01:04,848 --> 00:01:09,436
In the nineteen seventies, astronomers noticed something strange. Galaxies

19
00:01:09,436 --> 00:01:14,024
near us were not moving the way the expanding

20
00:01:14,024 --> 00:01:18,612
universe said they should. They were all flowing toward

21
00:01:18,612 --> 00:01:23,200
one spot, a region of sky near the constellations

22
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,788
Hydra and Centaurus. They called it the Great Attractor,

23
00:01:27,788 --> 00:01:32,376
because something enormous had to be doing the pulling.

24
00:01:33,876 --> 00:01:37,635
The Great Attractor sits about two hundred and fifty million light

25
00:01:37,635 --> 00:01:41,394
years away. That sounds far, but in cosmic terms it is

26
00:01:41,394 --> 00:01:45,153
practically our next door neighbor. Here is the problem. It hides

27
00:01:45,153 --> 00:01:48,912
behind the brightest, dustiest part of our own galaxy. Astronomers call

28
00:01:48,912 --> 00:01:52,671
that band the Zone of Avoidance, because it avoids being seen.

29
00:01:52,671 --> 00:01:56,430
Two hundred and fifty million years ago, when the light we

30
00:01:56,430 --> 00:02:00,189
see today began its journey, dinosaurs had not even appeared on

31
00:02:00,189 --> 00:02:03,948
Earth yet.

32
00:02:05,448 --> 00:02:09,164
Imagine trying to spot a campfire through a sandstorm, from behind a brick

33
00:02:09,164 --> 00:02:12,880
wall. That is what we are up against. The disk of the Milky

34
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:16,596
Way, full of gas and dust and blazing stars, blocks our view of

35
00:02:16,596 --> 00:02:20,312
whatever sits on the other side. It blows my mind that the most

36
00:02:20,312 --> 00:02:24,028
powerful thing in our cosmic neighborhood could be hiding behind our own front

37
00:02:24,028 --> 00:02:27,744
porch.

38
00:02:29,244 --> 00:02:33,316
Whatever it is, it is monstrous. Scientists estimate its mass at

39
00:02:33,316 --> 00:02:37,388
something like fifty thousand Milky Ways, or even more. An invisible

40
00:02:37,388 --> 00:02:41,460
fist of gravity, weighing more than some entire superclusters, sitting in

41
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:45,532
the dark and bending the paths of a hundred thousand galaxies

42
00:02:45,532 --> 00:02:49,604
around it. If you could weigh it on a cosmic scale,

43
00:02:49,604 --> 00:02:53,676
the needle would break. Nothing else in our neighborhood comes close.

44
00:02:55,176 --> 00:02:58,918
Then, in twenty fourteen, the picture got even bigger.

45
00:02:58,918 --> 00:03:02,659
Astronomer Brent Tully mapped the motions of thousands of

46
00:03:02,659 --> 00:03:06,401
galaxies and found that we all belong to one

47
00:03:06,401 --> 00:03:10,142
gigantic structure. He named it Laniakea, Hawaiian for immense

48
00:03:10,142 --> 00:03:13,884
heaven. Laniakea holds about one hundred thousand galaxies, spans

49
00:03:13,884 --> 00:03:17,626
five hundred million light years, and at the bottom

50
00:03:17,626 --> 00:03:21,367
of its gravitational valley sits the Great Attractor. The

51
00:03:21,367 --> 00:03:25,109
name comes from a Hawaiian phrase meaning immense heaven,

52
00:03:25,109 --> 00:03:28,850
and it fits. We are citizens of Laniakea, whether

53
00:03:28,850 --> 00:03:32,592
we knew it or not.

54
00:03:34,092 --> 00:03:37,928
Think of Laniakea like a mountain watershed. Rain that falls anywhere

55
00:03:37,928 --> 00:03:41,764
in a valley flows downhill into the same river. Galaxies are

56
00:03:41,764 --> 00:03:45,600
the raindrops, and the Great Attractor is the lowest point of

57
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:49,436
the valley. Everything in our corner of the universe is sliding

58
00:03:49,436 --> 00:03:53,272
downhill toward it. We live inside that valley, and we never

59
00:03:53,272 --> 00:03:57,108
even knew.

60
00:03:58,608 --> 00:04:02,315
Peeking through gaps in the dust, telescopes have caught

61
00:04:02,315 --> 00:04:06,021
glimpses of what might be there. A colossal swarm

62
00:04:06,021 --> 00:04:09,728
of galaxies called the Norma Cluster, and behind it

63
00:04:09,728 --> 00:04:13,435
even more hidden mass. But here is the terrifying

64
00:04:13,435 --> 00:04:17,141
part. Everything we can see is still not heavy

65
00:04:17,141 --> 00:04:20,848
enough. The pulling is stronger than the visible stuff

66
00:04:20,848 --> 00:04:24,555
can explain. The numbers refuse to add up. Something

67
00:04:24,555 --> 00:04:28,261
else is there. Something heavy, something dark, and something

68
00:04:28,261 --> 00:04:31,968
patient.

69
00:04:33,468 --> 00:04:37,318
Just think about this for a second. There is something

70
00:04:37,318 --> 00:04:41,169
out there, two hundred and fifty million light years away,

71
00:04:41,169 --> 00:04:45,019
dragging one hundred thousand galaxies toward it, and after fifty

72
00:04:45,019 --> 00:04:48,869
years of looking, we still do not know what it

73
00:04:48,869 --> 00:04:52,719
is. It might be the biggest structure we have ever

74
00:04:52,719 --> 00:04:56,570
found. Or it might be something physics has not even

75
00:04:56,570 --> 00:05:00,420
named yet.

76
00:05:08,430 --> 00:05:12,126
The leading suspect is dark matter, the invisible scaffolding of the

77
00:05:12,126 --> 00:05:15,822
universe. We cannot see it, we cannot touch it, but its

78
00:05:15,822 --> 00:05:19,518
gravity holds galaxies together. The Great Attractor might be the densest

79
00:05:19,518 --> 00:05:23,214
knot of dark matter for hundreds of millions of light years

80
00:05:23,214 --> 00:05:26,910
in every direction. A shadow mountain range we can only feel,

81
00:05:26,910 --> 00:05:30,606
never photograph. It makes up most of the gravity in the

82
00:05:30,606 --> 00:05:34,302
universe, yet no one has ever seen a single particle of

83
00:05:34,302 --> 00:05:37,998
it directly.

84
00:05:39,498 --> 00:05:43,386
But wait, it gets weirder. In two thousand eight, a NASA

85
00:05:43,386 --> 00:05:47,274
team studying the afterglow of the Big Bang found hints that

86
00:05:47,274 --> 00:05:51,162
the Great Attractor itself is moving. Whole clusters of galaxies, including

87
00:05:51,162 --> 00:05:55,050
ours, might be caught in an even bigger current called dark

88
00:05:55,050 --> 00:05:58,938
flow, streaming toward something beyond the edge of what we can

89
00:05:58,938 --> 00:06:02,826
ever see. If that is true, then the Great Attractor is

90
00:06:02,826 --> 00:06:06,714
not the final answer. It is just another leaf in an

91
00:06:06,714 --> 00:06:10,602
even bigger river.

92
00:06:12,102 --> 00:06:15,722
Beyond the observable universe. Sit with that for a moment.

93
00:06:15,722 --> 00:06:19,342
The pull might come from a structure so far away

94
00:06:19,342 --> 00:06:22,962
that its light will never reach us, something outside the

95
00:06:22,962 --> 00:06:26,582
part of reality we are allowed to observe. It blows

96
00:06:26,582 --> 00:06:30,202
my mind that the universe might have neighbors we are

97
00:06:30,202 --> 00:06:33,822
forbidden from ever meeting.

98
00:06:35,322 --> 00:06:38,904
New telescopes are fighting back. X-ray observatories and radio

99
00:06:38,904 --> 00:06:42,486
surveys are mapping the hidden zone piece by piece,

100
00:06:42,486 --> 00:06:46,068
finding galaxy clusters we never knew existed. Each discovery

101
00:06:46,068 --> 00:06:49,650
adds mass to the map. And each time, the

102
00:06:49,650 --> 00:06:53,232
Great Attractor seems a little less lonely, and a

103
00:06:53,232 --> 00:06:56,814
little more terrifying. Slowly, painstakingly, the invisible is becoming

104
00:06:56,814 --> 00:07:00,396
visible. But every answer seems to bring a bigger

105
00:07:00,396 --> 00:07:03,978
question with it.

106
00:07:05,478 --> 00:07:09,456
So what happens when we finally get there? Nothing dramatic, sadly for

107
00:07:09,456 --> 00:07:13,434
the movies. Two hundred and fifty million light years is so far

108
00:07:13,434 --> 00:07:17,412
that the universe will keep expanding around us the whole way down.

109
00:07:17,412 --> 00:07:21,390
By the time we arrived, if we ever could, the Great Attractor

110
00:07:21,390 --> 00:07:25,368
would have merged and grown into something new. The journey is the

111
00:07:25,368 --> 00:07:29,346
story, not the destination. Galaxies will merge, stars will be born and

112
00:07:29,346 --> 00:07:33,324
die, and the river will keep flowing long after our sun has

113
00:07:33,324 --> 00:07:37,302
burned out.

114
00:07:38,802 --> 00:07:42,589
When I look up at the night sky now,

115
00:07:42,589 --> 00:07:46,376
I do not just see stars. I see a

116
00:07:46,376 --> 00:07:50,164
river. Our galaxy, our sun, our little Earth, all

117
00:07:50,164 --> 00:07:53,951
riding an invisible current toward a mystery we may

118
00:07:53,951 --> 00:07:57,738
never solve. It blows my mind that we are

119
00:07:57,738 --> 00:08:01,525
passengers on the largest journey in our part of

120
00:08:01,525 --> 00:08:05,312
the universe, and most of us never feel a

121
00:08:05,312 --> 00:08:09,100
thing. A current wider than a hundred thousand galaxies,

122
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:12,887
older than the Earth itself, carrying us all toward

123
00:08:12,887 --> 00:08:16,674
the unknown.

124
00:08:18,174 --> 00:08:21,755
If this gave you chills, you are in

125
00:08:21,755 --> 00:08:25,337
the right place. I tell a new cosmic

126
00:08:25,337 --> 00:08:28,918
mystery like this every single day, so hit

127
00:08:28,918 --> 00:08:32,499
subscribe and turn on notifications, because the universe

128
00:08:32,499 --> 00:08:36,081
keeps getting stranger, and you do not want

129
00:08:36,081 --> 00:08:39,662
to miss what we find next. Every day

130
00:08:39,662 --> 00:08:43,243
brings a new mystery, a new wonder, a

131
00:08:43,243 --> 00:08:46,825
new reason to look up and ask what

132
00:08:46,825 --> 00:08:50,406
else is hiding out there.

133
00:08:51,906 --> 00:08:55,848
The Great Attractor is still out there, still pulling, still hiding. Maybe one

134
00:08:55,848 --> 00:08:59,790
day we will see its face. Until then, keep looking up, and keep

135
00:08:59,790 --> 00:09:03,732
wondering. This is Cosmic Unknown, and I will see you in the next

136
00:09:03,732 --> 00:09:07,674
